  recorded against the baseline, and they are:
  `engine/reports/M3_TOKENS.txt`).

**27/27 tests green.** Next: M4 — serialization and portability (TK13).

## 2026-07-20: 018 — Milestone M4 passed; Stage 4 gates wired; ledger complete

**M4 — serialization and portability (TK13).** `CellRecord` implemented
per the D09 wire-format contract: the agent grammar is the carrier
(version-headed single-cell fragment, origin hash when settled, canonical
shortest-round-trip numbers). Six-test harness, all passing first run:

- Cross-layout injection: export from settled dashboard, inject into
  settled settings, re-settle — bit-identical across repeated runs. The
  cross-surface portability claim is now a **measured result** (EV04),
  not internal reasoning.
- VG02 schema gate: field set equals the whitelist exactly;
  geometry-bearing records are rejected at parse.
- Binds do not travel; smuggled bind ops are rejected.
- Canonical form is a fixpoint (export→inject→export is stable).
- **Position cannot leak**: identically-declared cells at different
  settled positions export byte-identical records — the wire-level proof
  that geometry is structurally absent.
- Duplicate injection is rejected atomically.

**Stage 4 — automated gates (TK14/TK15/TK16, plus TK03).**
`engine/tests/gates.rs` adds the frame-budget gate (50 regions inside
16 ms/frame during active settling) and API-boundary determinism.
`.githooks/pre-commit` (wired via `core.hooksPath`) runs the full 35-test
engine suite, the MIVIM link-completeness audit (tool now permanent at
`.MIVIM/tools/mivim_audit.py`), and markdownlint before every commit —
a failing invariant is drift by definition and blocks the commit. Gate
verified green end-to-end.

**The execution ledger is complete**: all 16 tasks done, all 12
requirements covered, milestone order held throughout (M1→M2→M3→M4, each
gated on the prior binary exit), 35/35 tests green. Workstream A — the
agent-facing API that is the point of the system — exists, validated.

## 2026-07-20: 019 — Stage 5: the integration demo (TK17–TK19)

The visible demo, built on top of the completed API (harness-first
condition satisfied). Ledger extended with Stage 5 (WL06, REQ13/REQ14,
TK17–TK19; 79/79 link-complete) before any code, per the pipeline.

- **The real engine in the browser**: `demo/` compiles the engine to a
  195 KB WASM module with hand-rolled glue — the dependency-free property
  carries all the way to the demo. The page drives layout exclusively
  through the public API and renders only what `Snapshot` exposes
  (REQ13): centroid, hull, settled, strain, vessel map. Content is the
  four agent-authored programs (R3 dashboard + the three M3 intents),
  shown alongside their natural-language intents.
- **Verification earned its keep twice** (REQ14, the standing rule):
  1. The first render drew discrete dots — the exact analysis/003 drift,
     again — and passed the programmatic checks because they didn't test
     connectedness. Caught by *looking at the screenshot*; fixed with a
     filled-hull renderer (one connected blob per cell by construction)
     and encoded permanently: the verifier now requires one connected
     component per cell with every centroid on matter.
  2. The flicker check failed on one layout: engine strain was exactly
     zero (verified natively and in-browser) — the culprit was GPU raster
     nondeterminism under canvas blur. Fixed structurally: the renderer
     skips repainting unchanged state, so a settled raster is literally
     frozen.
- Final verification, all four layouts: settled at ~300 ms wall-time,
  strain exactly 0.000, one connected component per cell, raster frozen
  over a one-second window. `demo/verify_demo.py` is the repeatable gate;
  screenshots regenerable, not tracked.

The full pipeline — brainstorm → paper → prototype → engine → **living
demo driven by agent-authored programs** — is now walkable end to end.

## 2026-07-20: 020 — Intent correction: the demo becomes the framework

Creative-lead intervention: the first demo showed floating blobs — plumbing
presented as thesis, the box model in a slime costume. Root cause: three
mechanisms specified in the frozen paper were never realized in the
prototype solver, and the master developer had lost the intent picture
(solid interface at rest; fluid only in transition; one substance filling
the vessel; cells ARE their expressed content). Corrected against the
standards — every change is a discrete realization of frozen theory:

- **E_fill realized** (eq. 6): layout worlds fill the vessel — outward
  quasi-static drive (C2) that yields against S, ξ rebuild suspended
  during the fill phase (§7.2 boundary condition). The vessel is claimed;
  **sizes are solved from material**: `S:0.3 main` settles at 2.4× the
  default-S KPI cells, exactly the §9 worked example.
- **Post-yield plastic flow** (n<1 realization): awake layout regions'
  material rest lengths follow realized geometry at a rate yielding
  against S — soft matter flows into claimed space, stiff keeps shape,
  rests hold elastically at rest.
- **Binds transport bulk** (E_cohesion): a single-anchor spring failed —
  plasticity absorbed it while the body never moved; and a
  stiffness-scaled attraction slingshotted at rest (compliance-at-rest
  failure class, third sighting). Final mechanism: bounded interfacial
  drive moving all particles of both regions until adjacency; stiff
  contacts stop overlap — cohesive groups pack, solved.
- **§7.3 latch assignment**: the region latch now literally sets ξ=1 on
  engage (as the paper writes it) — caught by the M4 position-leak test
  when settle *history* leaked into exported records via divergent ξ.
- **Registry-complete API**: `Engine::register_asset` feeds real bytes
  through sequencing; the engine registers the R4 extension type, so
  cells genuinely express chart / player / reader / image-viewer.
- **§9 zero-cost adaptation, live**: `resize_vessel` reshapes the world
  to the vessel aspect, wakes the medium, and it re-settles with no
  program call — demonstrated and verified (program text unchanged).
- **Verification raised to the framework standard**: per layout — settled
  exact, vessel substantially claimed, big-cell ratio ≥1.5× satellites,
  bound pairs adjacent, phenotypes as expected, raster frozen at rest,
  plus the resize claim. All four layouts + resize PASS; engine suite
  35/35 green throughout.

Remaining gap, named honestly: the settled screen is a packed organic
tessellation, not yet a crisp edge-to-edge interface — corner voids
remain (~50–80% lit by layout), menisci are rendered rather than
E_cohesion-derived, and content inclusions are still the deferred
fidelity step. The framework claims are now TRUE on screen; the polish to
"solid interface at rest" is the next iteration.

## 2026-07-20: 021 — Stage 6 in progress: tessellation physics

Stage 6 (TK20–TK22) opened in the ledger and substantially advanced; exit
not yet met, recorded honestly rather than declared.

**Landed:**

- **Wall-coverage stall predicate**: the fill phase may not end while
  wall spans remain unclaimed (edge-to-edge is part of E_fill's
  definition). Boundary segments are tracked; stall requires >90% wall
  coverage plus growth stall, with a hard-stop safety. Screen claim rose
  from 50–75% to **92–100% lit**; chat and the §9 resize demonstration
  now pass the raised bar completely at 100% lit.
- **Wall-band void-seeking**: near walls, fill direction blends toward
  the local void (creep along walls, corner zones owned by void-seeking)
  — restricted to the wall band because global void-seeking demonstrably
  pulls taffy shards (probed, rejected).
- **Verification raised** (TK21 partial): lit ≥ 80% plus a four-corner
  claim check; API settle budget raised to the invariant-suite budget.
- Mechanisms probed and rejected with evidence, per anti-drift: global
  voidward steering (shards), strong roundness tension (collapses fill,
  breaks solved sizes), per-frame area-target scaling (starves against
  constraint pullback — soft cells never grow).

**Open problems, precisely named for the next session:**

1. **Cell interleaving.** Dilated lattices are porous point-clouds —
   particle spacing grows to 2–3 units while contact radius stays 0.5,
   so regions finger through each other's interiors and their hulls
   overlap like stacked glass sheets instead of tiling. Identified fix,
   physically principled: effective particle radius must scale with
   lattice spacing (continuum matter does not become porous when it
   dilates). A first attempt destabilized contact rests, the C1 guard,
   and arrest balance across the suite — reverted; it needs its own
   instrumented iteration like the arrest mechanism did.
2. **Corner wedges** partially unclaimed (1–3 of 4 corners per layout at
   the 50 px / 30% standard) — likely resolved by the same radius fix,
   since non-porous cells under pressure must squeeze into wedges.

State at close: engine suite 8/8 groups green, merge gate exit 0, demo
verified at the previous framework standard with the two named
shortfalls. TK20/TK21 remain open with progress notes; TK22 (v0.3
addendum) deferred until the tessellation mechanics stop moving.

## 2026-07-20: 022 — Stage 6 continued: radius conservation lands

The identified fix from entry 021 implemented with its full support and
kept: **effective particle radius scales with lattice spacing**
(continuum matter does not become porous when it dilates), the C1 guard
tolerance made proportional to contact rest (an absolute tolerance
misclassifies ordinary prestress at dilated scale), the contact search
grid follows the radius, and the material radius is exposed through the
read path (`CellView.radius`) so the renderer draws true material
extent. Engine suite 8/8 green with the mechanism live; lit fraction now
**95–100% on every layout**; chat and the §9 resize pass the tessellation
bar completely.

Honest residual, sharpened by this iteration: interleaving is reduced
but not eliminated — shard arms frozen during early growth cross other
cells, and a per-region MEAN radius cannot seal non-uniformly stretched
arms. Corners follow the same cause. Both trace to the one paper term
still unrealized: **E_cohesion's interface tension** (compactness /
interface-length minimization). Naive versions were already probed and
rejected with evidence (global void-seeking → shards; radial roundness
tension → collapses fill). Per the pipeline — planning before
development — the next step is TK22: design the discrete E_cohesion
mechanism on paper as part of the v0.3 addendum, then implement against
that design.

## 2026-07-20: 023 — TK22 complete: the v0.3 addendum

`latex/v0.3/NNUI_CELL_Addendum_v0.3.tex` (+ built PDF), supplementing the
v0.2 specification, which remains canonical. Contents:

- **Corrections C5–C9, validated and normative**, each with its measured
  failure lineage: C5 E_fill as a quasi-static drive with the
  wall-coverage stall predicate (edge-to-edge is part of E_fill's
  definition; area-target stall measured at 50–75% claim vs 95–100%
  with the predicate); C6 plastic post-yield rest adaptation (the
  discrete n<1 realization); C7 bulk-transport binding (single-anchor
  springs absorbed by plasticity; stiffness-scaled attraction slingshots
  at rest — third sighting of the compliance-at-rest failure class); C8
  the latch's exact ξ=1 assignment (settle history must not leak into
  portable records); C9 radius conservation under dilation with
  proportional C1 tolerance and radius exposed through the read path.
- **The E_cohesion design** — the addendum's core new content, marked
  honestly as design (internal reasoning), not result: interface tension
  realized as **discrete curvature flow on region boundaries**. Boundary
  particles detected by neighborhood deficit; discrete curvature κ_i =
  (mean of boundary neighbors) − x_i; bounded quasi-static tension step
  along κ. Flat interfaces (wall contact, shared seams) carry no force —
  so it cannot fight tiling or wall conformity, which is precisely where
  both failed candidates died; bulges retract, dents fill, shard arms
  collapse. Predicted equilibrium: compact tiles, flat shared seams,
  menisci — the paper's own stated E_cohesion consequences.
- **Pre-registered binary exits T1–T4** gate the implementation: no
  interleaving (pairwise hull intersection <2%), all corners claimed,
  nothing regresses (claim ≥90%, sizes, binds, phenotypes, frozen rest,
  §9 resize), full suite green with determinism preserved. The design is
  wrong if they fail, and the failure record joins the lineage.

Stage 6 next step is now specified work, not exploration: implement
boundary curvature flow against T1–T4.

## 2026-07-20: 024 — Curvature flow implemented; T1 and T4 pass; T2/T3 open

The E_cohesion design implemented exactly as specified (boundary
detection by neighbour deficit, discrete curvature as the boundary
Laplacian, bounded quasi-static tension step) and run against the
pre-registered exits. The contract worked as intended — it caught a real
phenomenon the design had not anticipated and stopped an over-claimed
result:

- **Engulfment discovered.** With compactness active, fast soft cells
  wrap *around* slower siblings through the void beside them (particle
  contacts act only at touching surfaces; the bulk never pushes).
  Measured: a gallery thumbnail 100% inside main's hull. Foam physics
  permits nesting; sibling cells must tile. Added the **sibling
  exclusion** — a region-level topological constraint (C1 class): a
  centroid-distance floor resolved as bounded bulk translation.
- **T1 PASS** (engine-level, all four layouts): pairwise settled-hull
  intersection < 2% of the smaller cell — the interleaving defect that
  triggered this whole line of work is dead. New permanent test:
  `engine/tests/t_cohesion.rs`.
- **T4 PASS**: full suite 9/9 groups green with curvature flow, sibling
  exclusion, and all prior mechanisms live; determinism preserved; merge
  gate exit 0.
- **T2/T3 FAIL, recorded**: vessel claim dropped to 46–65% on
  gallery/chat (from 95–100% pre-cohesion) and corners remain partial.
  Diagnosis: the exclusion needed against engulfment couples against the
  fill — it blocks packing, the stall hard-cap fires, the vessel
  underfills. Two alternative exclusion geometries (face-contact sum,
  anti-nesting max) were probed and destabilized other exits; iteration
  stopped per the anti-roulette rule.

State kept in tree: the T1-green configuration (correctness property won;
fill quantity open). The open problem for the next design pass is precise:
**an exclusion that forbids nesting without forbidding packing** — likely
requiring the exclusion to act on hull containment rather than centroid
distance, or the fill stall to distinguish exclusion-blocked from
genuinely-jammed. Per the addendum contract, this failure record joins
the C-series lineage.
